t an analogous Gibbs-style statistical ensemble formalism valid for many-body systems arbitrarily far from equilibrium. Specifically:\n\n1. Identify a class of nonequilibrium statistical operators ρ(t) on Γ, parameterized by a set of macroscopic observables {P_j(t)} (informational basis), such that ρ(t) is determined by an extremum principle (e.g. maximization of an information-theoretic entropy S[ρ] = −Tr(ρ ln ρ) under the constraints Tr[ρ(t) P_j] = ⟨P_j⟩(t) together with appropriate retroeffect / initial-condition conditions encoding irreversibility).\n\n2. Show that the resulting ensembles are compatible with the underlying microscopic Liouville (or von Neumann) evolution, in the sense that the evolution equations for ⟨P_j⟩(t) are derivable from the microdynamics.\n\n3. From this ensemble, systematically derive: (i) the laws of irreversible thermodynamics (entropy production, H-theorem); (ii) closed thermo-hydrodynamic equations for the relevant macrovariables; (iii) a nonlinear kinetic theory for the one-particle distribution function; (iv) generalized response functions and fluctuation–dissipation relations valid out of equilibrium; (v) explicit expressions for transport coefficients.\n\n4. Characterize the correct choice of informational basis {P_j} and provide criteria under which the resulting ensemble is unique and uniquely encodes the irreversible macroscopic behavior of the system.\n\nThe overall question is whether such a Gibbs-style nonequilibrium ensemble formalism exists with the same logical status, generality, and predictive completeness as the equilibrium Gibbs ensembles, and if so to specify it explicitly.","status_search_result":"The construction of a fully satisfactory Gibbs-style ensemble theory for arbitrary nonequilibrium systems remains a long-standing open foundational problem in statistical mechanics. Several partial frameworks exist (Zubanev's NSO/MaxEnt-NESOM, Mori–Zwanzig projection operators, GENERIC, steady-state thermodynamics, large